        "paragraphs": [
          "To describe the exact electronic state, energy, orbital geometry, and spin orientation of an electron in an atom, wave mechanics uses four quantum numbers:",
          "1. Principal Quantum Number (n): Defines the primary energy level or shell (K, L, M, N... for n = 1, 2, 3, 4...). Specifies overall size and distance from nucleus.",
          "   • Shell orbital capacity = n²",
          "   • Maximum electron capacity = 2n²",
          "2. Azimuthal / Subsidiary Quantum Number (l): Specifies the subshell type and 3D geometric shape of the orbital. For a given n, l ranges from 0 to (n − 1).",
          "   • l = 0 (s, spherical), l = 1 (p, dumbbell), l = 2 (d, double dumbbell), l = 3 (f, complex).",
          "   • Determines Orbital Angular Momentum: L = √(l(l+1)) h / (2π) = √(l(l+1)) ℏ.",
          "3. Magnetic Quantum Number (mₗ): Defines the spatial orientation of the orbital in 3D relative to external magnetic fields. For a given l, mₗ ranges from −l to +l (total 2l + 1 values).",
          "   • l = 0 (s): mₗ = 0 (1 orientation)",
          "   • l = 1 (p): mₗ = −1, 0, +1 (3 orientations: p_x, p_y, p_z)",
          "   • l = 2 (d): mₗ = −2, −1, 0, +1, +2 (5 orientations: d_xy, d_yz, d_zx, d_x²-y², d_z²)",
          "4. Spin Quantum Number (m⛥): Describes intrinsic electron spin direction along a magnetic axis. Takes only two values: +1/2 (spin-up ↑) or −1/2 (spin-down ↓)."
        ]
