    "chapter": "Structure of Atom",
    "whatIsThisTopic": "Once the structure of atomic orbitals and their quantum numbers are established, the central question in chemistry becomes: How are electrons distributed among available orbitals in ground-state atoms and ions? \n\nThe distribution of electrons in orbitals is called the Electronic Configuration of an atom. Electron placement is governed by three fundamental principles:\n1. The Aufbau Principle (and the (n + l) energy rule) — determines the order in which orbitals are filled by increasing energy.\n2. The Pauli Exclusion Principle — dictates that an orbital can hold a maximum of two electrons, and they must possess opposite spins.\n3. Hund's Rule of Maximum Multiplicity — governs how degenerate (equal energy) orbitals are occupied to maximize total electron spin and minimize inter-electronic repulsion.\n\nMastering these rules enables students to write ground-state configurations for neutral atoms and transition-metal cations, understand exceptional stability in Chromium (Cr) and Copper (Cu), count unpaired electrons, and predict paramagnetic vs diamagnetic properties in NEET.",
    "basicIdea": [
      "Where do electrons actually go inside an atom?",
      "Electrons do not fly randomly around the nucleus. They occupy specific quantum energy levels according to a strict organizational hierarchy:",
      "ATOM ➔ SHELL (n) ➔ SUBSHELL (l) ➔ ORBITAL (mₗ) ➔ ELECTRON SPIN (m⛥)",
      "• Shell (n = 1, 2, 3...): Major energy level (K, L, M, N...).",
      "• Subshell (l = 0, 1, 2, 3): Sub-division defining orbital shape (s, p, d, f).",
      "• Orbital (mₗ = −l to +l): A 3D spatial region that can hold at most TWO electrons.",
      "• Electron Spin (m⛥ = +1/2 or −1/2): Two paired electrons in the same orbital MUST spin in opposite directions (↑↓).",
      "Electrons fill orbitals starting from the lowest energy level available, filling singly before pairing up."
    ],
