        "paragraphs": [
          "• Enthalpy Change (Δ_r H): During a chemical reaction occurring at constant temperature and pressure, heat is exchanged with the surroundings. The enthalpy change of reaction is defined as:",
          "  Δ_r H = H_final (products) − H_initial (reactants) = Σ H(products) − Σ H(reactants)",
          "• Exothermic Reactions:",
          "  - Energy is RELEASED by the system into surroundings (H_products < H_reactants).",
          "  - Enthalpy change is NEGATIVE: Δ_r H < 0.",
          "  - Example: Combustion of methane gas: CH₄(g) + 2 O₂(g) ➔ CO₂(g) + 2 H₂O(l), Δ_r H = −890.4 kJ mol⁻¹.",
          "  - Reaction Profile: Reactants sit on a higher enthalpy level than products; the downward energy step represents released heat.",
          "• Endothermic Reactions:",
          "  - Energy is ABSORBED by the system from surroundings (H_products > H_reactants).",
          "  - Enthalpy change is POSITIVE: Δ_r H > 0.",
          "  - Example: Decomposition of calcium carbonate: CaCO₃(s) ➔ CaO(s) + CO₂(g), Δ_r H = +178.3 kJ mol⁻¹.",
          "  - Reaction Profile: Reactants sit on a lower enthalpy level than products; the upward energy step represents absorbed heat."
        ]
